ature just above t1 and now all of  the PCR products one targets are going  to melt and by melt I mean their  fluorescence is going to go away you can  see the blue dots have gone away so in  reality that would be a little bit of  green fluorescence going away in any  partition where I saw a decrease in  green fluorescence those partitions had  PCR product one alright so now I can  take that same array increase the  temperature just pass t2 and I'm gonna  see my yellow PCR product go away  so any difference in fluorescence here  I'm gonna say that partition at PCR  product number two I think you know  where I'm going next we're gonna raise  the temperature just past e3 and now I'm  gonna see any partitions with  fluorescence left over that signal  should now go away and those partitions  contain the PCR product three and it's  actually already been shown that you can  train a computer to recognize up to a  hundred different melt curve signatures  if you monitor those melt curves with  high enough resolution so you can see  this very messy overwhelming plots down  here in the right corner if the aren't  very human to make out the differences  there but a computer can do that for us  apologies  and if you were wondering combinati has  already done some proof of concept  experiments to show that we can do this  this particular slide is showing ABC are  able assay for chronic myeloid leukemia  recurrence monitoring so VCR Abel is a  fusion transcript that is very common  it's it's a signature of chronic myeloid  leukemia and often physicians want to  monitor both the BCR Abel fusion and  also the wild-type Abel and so in this  particular assay there were four  possibilities so if I run this assay  with and I want to quantify wild-type  Abel and BCR Abel and I run the sample  out in a array the partitions in that  array are either going to be negative  for both targets they're going to have  wild-type Abel target or they're gonna  have the BCR Abel fusion target or  they're gonna have both targets and so  what we need to be able to do is  distinguish between these three  possibilities and these three bottom  graphs are examples of these so these  curves were generated from individual  digital PCR partitions so these are very  small partitions where we can monitor  the fluorescence and generate curves for  the fluorescence decay and you can see  that we can easily distinguish between a  partition that has wild-type Abel a  partition that has a bcr-abl fusion and  a partition that has both and I suppose  I should have actually added to this  slide one more chart showing just a flat  line for a partition that has none of  these three so you'll just have to  believe me that that's indeed what it  does look like and with that that  concludes my presentation and so I hope  I've convinced you that multiplexed  monitoring of miniscule circulating  tumor DNA biomarkers through array based  you'll PCR truly holds the key to better  patient outcomes and better cancer  discoveries and yeah thank you for your  time I welcome any and all questions  feel free to submit your questions and I  will get back to you via email as  quickly as I can and we're also open to  collaborations if you happen to be a  researching some sort of cancer or any  other technology or science that would  be benefited from digital PCR all right  I hope to hear from some of you guys and  thank you so much
 

